#basePath P1_NativeLamprey
#include "Header.leaf"

:: Start
@exposed
@trigger    OnLocationChanged
// "NONE" ensures no other threads are in-progress
// global:currentPhase == 1 ensures player is in phase 1
@when       global:currentThread == "NONE", global:currentPhase == 1, location == "DNROffice"
@cutscene
// Lock all location on map
$call SetInThreadLocks()
$call DisableMapButton()
$call SetBackground("bg/dnr-office-bg.png")

// Mark start of thread
$set global:currentThread = "P1_NativeLamprey"

$call  ClearVisibleLines()

{@}     
       The DNR office is nicely furnished with natural memorabilia, a straight-laced office man sitting behind the desk.

{@Paul}
       Mm, hello and welcome to the Department of Natural Resources. My name is Paul. How can I help you?

{@You}
       Um, hi, Paul. I'm from Sea Grant and was wondering if you could tell me a bit about the indigenous range of sea lamprey. 

{@}
       You show him the photo of the bite mark. 
       He stares at it like it's a tax return and not a fish with a hole in its side. 

{@Paul}
       Mm, yes, sea lamprey. They evolved in the Atlantic Ocean. Mhm.
       But in the 1800s, newly-built canals allowed the sea lamprey to reach the Great Lakes.
       The sea lamprey and unsustainable fishing practices caused the population of other fish species to sharply decline. 

{@You}
       How come?
       
{@Paul}
       Mm. The fish that evolved alongisde the sea lamprey have defense mechanisms when bit.
       But fish in the Great Lakes don't, so they are more likely to die.
       Sea lamprey as a species aren't the issue. It's the fact that they were introduced into a struggling environment.
       
{@You}
       So why is this becoming a new issue now? I mean, the introduction was two hundred years ago. 

{@Paul}
       Hm. There are many techniques that have successfully been used to control the sea lamprey population.
       But climate change has put the ecosystem here under stress.
       Plus, during the pandemic, the control methods were stopped.

{@You}
       So it's an issue of restarting those techniques to manage the population?
       Why not just try to, like, remove them all? Weren't things better in the past?

{@}
       He tilts his head at you, dark eyes set quizzicaly in a bored face. 

{@Paul}
       Mm. I've been here a long time. I've seen things come and go. 
       The future is always a gift. It is worth striving for. 

{@}
       You blink at him. He might have smiled, but it's hard to tell. 

{@Paul}
       But to answer your question, no, we are not trying to remove all the sea lamprey. The point is to create ecological balance for all. 

{@}
       You think about cool lake water lapping at your toes as a child. Of summers on boats and piers. 
       The photo of the bitten fish burns a hole in your pocket. 

{@You}
       I see. Thanks for your help, Paul. 

{@Paul}
       Mm. Of course. Come back any time. 
       And check out the Lampricide Field Station to look at restarting control methods. 
       
$call GiveEvidenceChip("Evidence-native-lamprey")

{@}
       This will be useful.

// skip to end
$goto .End


:: End
@exposed
@tracked
// Mark end of thread
$set global:currentThread = "NONE"

$call OpenMap()

// Go to Phase 1 Continue Hub
$goto P1_Hub.Continue